{
 "title": "Panel regression for GDP of CEE countries using time-varying coefficients",
 "hypothesis": "H1 effects vary over time around 2008 and COVID, H2 private debt strong adverse post-2008, H3 private debt explains Baltic divergence; supported by beta_X7 coefficients and kappa Table1 and lambda(t) Figure6",
 "data_sources": [
  "Eurostat PPP/price/GDPpc PPS EU27=100",
  "World Bank FDI, trade, GCF",
  "ESTAT gov debt and private debt consolidated tipspd20",
  "IMF WEO April 2025 for rank illustration Figure2 not used in regression"
 ],
 "sample": "Panel of 11 EU-acceding CEE countries: Bulgaria, Czechia, Estonia, Croatia, Latvia, Lithuania, Hungary, Poland, Romania, Slovenia, Slovakia. Annual observations 1995-2024. Regressed variables matrix x is 11 countries x 29 time steps {1995,...,2023} x 7 factors. Dependent delta_y is 11 countries x 29 intervals 1996-2024. Total pooled observations 11*29=319. Sources: Eurostat PPP/price/GDPpc PPS EU27=100 and ESTAT gov debt and private debt consolidated, World Bank FDI net inflows %GDP, trade %GDP, GCF %GDP (X3 and X5 accumulated over period considered). Filters: period 1995-2024 only, as reliable CEE data available from 1995.",
 "period_start": "1995",
 "period_end": "2024",
 "method": [
  "1. Define delta_y_{i,t}=x_{i,t,1}-x_{i,t-1,1} as annual change in GDP per capita PPP PPS EU27=100. Regressor vector x_{i,t-1} = [X1 GDPpc PPS, X2 price level index EU27=100, X3 accumulated FDI cum.%GDP, X4 trade %GDP, X5 accumulated GCF cum.%GDP, X6 central gov debt %GDP, X7 private sector debt consolidated %GDP] lagged t-1.",
  "2. Pooled standardization: mu_j = 1/NT sum x_{i,t,j}, sigma_j^2 = 1/(NT-1) sum (x_{i,t,j}-mu_j)^2, z_{i,t,j}=(x_{i,t,j}-mu_j)/sigma_j with NT=319. Pooled correlation Omega = cor(z) eigendecomposition Omega = W Lambda W^T, W^T W=I, Lambda=diag(lambda_k). Orthogonal regressors p_{i,t,k}= sum_j W_{j,k} z_{i,t,j}. By construction 1/(NT-1) sum p_{i,t,k} p_{i,t,l}=lambda_k delta_kl.",
  "3. Estimate LRVC in orthogonal basis year-by-year cross-section OLS N=11: delta_y_{i,t}= alpha_t^P + sum_{k=1}^d gamma_{t-1,k} p_{i,t-1,k} + e_{i,t} for t=1996..2024 (29 regressions, d=7, df=n-d-1=3). Report R2, Adjusted R2=1-(1-R2)(n-1)/(n-d-1), residual SD, F-statistic per year.",
  "4. Back-transformation to original X-space: beta_{t-1,j}=1/sigma_j sum_k W_{j,k} gamma_{t-1,k}, alpha_t=alpha_t^P - sum_j mu_j beta_{t-1,j} i.e. beta=diag(sigma)^{-1} W gamma, alpha=alpha^P - mu^T beta. Standard errors by delta method from P-space covariance through linear transformation. t-significance stars *p<0.10 **p<0.05 ***p<0.01. VIFs for X1-X7 range thresholds referenced.",
  "5. Decomposition: delta_y_{i,t,j}=alpha_t/d + beta_{t-1,j} x_{i,t-1,j}, delta_y=sum_j delta_y_j. Cumulative component kappa_{i,t,j}=GDP_{1995}/d + sum_{tau=1996}^t delta_y_{i,tau,j}. Relative private debt lambda(t)=sgn(kappa_{i,t,7}) kappa_{i,t,7}^2 / sum_j kappa_{i,t,j}^2. Full regressed delta_y^{fr} replaces lagged x_{t-1,1} by accumulated delta_y. Three GDP paths: GDP Data(t)=GDP1995+sum delta_y^e, GDP Regr.(t)=GDP1995+sum delta_y^r, GDP Regr.Full(t)=GDP1995+sum delta_y^{fr}, accumulated error e^a_{i,t}=sum e_{i,tau}=GDP Regr.-GDP Data.",
  "6. Diagnostics on residuals e_{i,t}: pooled 319 and per-country 29 descriptive stats (mean, SD, median, skew, kurt, Q25, Q75, IQR, RMSE/MAE, IQR/SD), Jarque-Bera normality, Bartlett heteroskedasticity across 29 yearly cross-sections, variance ratio max/min."
 ],
